{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"agent_message","text":"**As of November 6, 2026:**\n\n**Conceptual examples—not regulatory tests:** Mutual-fund cash redemptions may generate trading costs, cash drag and taxable gains shared by ETF shareholders. Conversely, ETF cash creations/redemptions or basket-related portfolio trading may impose costs on mutual-fund shareholders. ETF in-kind redemptions can remove low-basis securities, benefiting both classes; shared benefits alone do not establish improper subsidization.\n\n**Representative, order-specific monitoring framework:**\n- **Transaction costs:** Identify costs attributable to each class’s subscriptions/redemptions, their allocation, and whether one class bears another’s costs.\n- **Cash:** Assess cash generated or required by each class’s flows, associated balances and performance drag.\n- **Gains:** Evaluate realized gains attributable to class activity, resulting distributions, and tax benefits from in-kind redemptions.\n\nThe adviser and board assess cross-class effects and appropriate mitigation under the applicable exemptive order. Attribution methods, reporting cadence and intervention triggers must follow that order—not invented universal numerical thresholds."}}
